REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w01","title":"Figures of speech, discourse markers, rhythm, intonation and language use","summary":"Sixteen figures of speech that make English vivid, the discourse markers that hold talk together, how rhythm, pitch and intonation carry meaning in speech, and how English lives in multilingual India.","n":1,"lectures":[{"code":"1.1","title":"Figures of speech - part 1 | mastering similes, metaphors & more for creative communication","video":"SrYTLUUfrpQ","seconds":1736},{"code":"1.2","title":"Figures of speech- part 2 | alliteration, irony, puns & more for powerful expression","video":"zr4aHZ33UHA","seconds":1837},{"code":"1.3","title":"Discourse markers in speech | using so, well, you know & actually for fluent communication","video":"gEzyD_f706w","seconds":1969},{"code":"1.4","title":"Rhythm & pitch in english | mastering prosody for natural and powerful communication","video":"aJhf0Vp9040","seconds":2257},{"code":"1.5","title":"Intonation in english | mastering pitch, stress & rhythm for clear communication","video":"uE_v3pZFFe0","seconds":1214},{"code":"1.6","title":"Language use | exploring india’s linguistic diversity & cultural identity","video":"0XfkFlWWZtk","seconds":1497}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"what-is-a-figure-of-speech","level":2,"title":"What is a figure of speech?"},{"id":"figures-of-speech-part-1","level":2,"title":"Figures of speech, part 1"},{"id":"simile","level":3,"title":"Simile"},{"id":"metaphor","level":3,"title":"Metaphor"},{"id":"personification","level":3,"title":"Personification"},{"id":"paradox","level":3,"title":"Paradox"},{"id":"oxymoron","level":3,"title":"Oxymoron"},{"id":"antithesis","level":3,"title":"Antithesis"},{"id":"alliteration","level":3,"title":"Alliteration"},{"id":"figures-of-speech-part-2","level":2,"title":"Figures of speech, part 2"},{"id":"assonance-consonance-and-sibilance","level":3,"title":"Assonance, consonance and sibilance"},{"id":"irony","level":3,"title":"Irony"},{"id":"pun","level":3,"title":"Pun"},{"id":"juxtaposition","level":3,"title":"Juxtaposition"},{"id":"synecdoche","level":3,"title":"Synecdoche"},{"id":"anaphora","level":3,"title":"Anaphora"},{"id":"metonymy","level":3,"title":"Metonymy"},{"id":"litotes","level":3,"title":"Litotes"},{"id":"hyperbole","level":3,"title":"Hyperbole"},{"id":"euphemism","level":3,"title":"Euphemism"},{"id":"which-figure-is-it-a-quick-route","level":2,"title":"Which figure is it? A quick route"},{"id":"discourse-markers","level":2,"title":"Discourse markers"},{"id":"what-discourse-is","level":3,"title":"What discourse is"},{"id":"what-discourse-markers-are","level":3,"title":"What discourse markers are"},{"id":"a-conversation-with-and-without-markers","level":3,"title":"A conversation with and without markers"},{"id":"markers-by-use","level":3,"title":"Markers by use"},{"id":"rhythm","level":2,"title":"Rhythm"},{"id":"rhythm-means-flow","level":3,"title":"Rhythm means flow"},{"id":"english-is-stress-timed","level":3,"title":"English is stress-timed"},{"id":"stress-changes-meaning","level":3,"title":"Stress changes meaning"},{"id":"rhythm-in-poems-and-songs","level":3,"title":"Rhythm in poems and songs"},{"id":"pitch","level":2,"title":"Pitch"},{"id":"intonation","level":2,"title":"Intonation"},{"id":"five-related-terms","level":3,"title":"Five related terms"},{"id":"rising-falling-and-flat","level":3,"title":"Rising, falling and flat"},{"id":"what-intonation-does","level":3,"title":"What intonation does"},{"id":"language-use","level":2,"title":"Language use"},{"id":"india-s-language-families","level":3,"title":"India’s language families"},{"id":"languages-borrow-and-mix","level":3,"title":"Languages borrow and mix"},{"id":"language-in-school-three-myths","level":3,"title":"Language in school: three myths"},{"id":"english-in-india","level":3,"title":"English in India"},{"id":"indian-english","level":3,"title":"Indian English"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"},{"id":"which-figure-of-speech-is-this","level":3,"title":"Which figure of speech is this?"},{"id":"discourse-markers-rhythm-and-intonation","level":3,"title":"Discourse markers, rhythm and intonation"},{"id":"language-use-2","level":3,"title":"Language use"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>figure of speech</strong> is a literary device that says an idea indirectly, not literally, to give it more impact. Lectures 1.1 and 1.2 teach sixteen: simile, metaphor, personification, paradox, oxymoron, antithesis, alliteration (with its three forms: assonance, consonance and sibilance), irony, pun, juxtaposition, synecdoche, anaphora, metonymy, litotes, hyperbole and euphemism.</li>\n<li>To tell them apart, ask <strong>what links the two things</strong>: likeness (simile, metaphor), part and whole (synecdoche), a close relation (metonymy), opposites (oxymoron, paradox, antithesis, juxtaposition), repeated sounds (the alliteration family), or a gap between the words and the truth (irony, hyperbole, litotes, euphemism).</li>\n<li><strong>Discourse markers</strong> (1.3) such as <em>so, well, you know, actually, anyway</em> are connectors and fillers. They begin, link, shift, stress, clarify, contrast and close what we say, and they signal our opinion before we give it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rhythm</strong> (1.4) is the repeated pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables. English is <strong>stress-timed</strong> (“tum-te-tum”), and stressing a different word changes the meaning of the same sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Pitch</strong> (1.4) is how high or low the voice is; it depends on how fast the vocal cords vibrate. It shows our feelings, and a balanced, varied pitch keeps us from sounding monotonous.</li>\n<li><strong>Intonation</strong> (1.5) is the rise and fall of pitch: <strong>rising, falling or flat</strong>. A rise at the end can turn “John is a doctor” into a question. Intonation works at five levels: grammatical, attitudinal, discourse, focus and psychological.</li>\n<li><strong>Language use</strong> (1.6): India has five language families; languages borrow words and rules from each other; three common myths about learning languages are false; Indian English has its own features and is not “wrong”. The one rule: the more the input, the better the output.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-a-figure-of-speech\">What is a figure of speech?</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“He is very modest” gives information. “He is as modest as a hermit” paints a picture, and the listener remembers it. That is the job of a <strong>figure of speech</strong>: it says an idea in an indirect way so that the language has more <strong>impact</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Literal</strong> means “exactly what the words say”. Figures of speech are <strong>not</strong> meant literally: the man is not a hermit.</li>\n<li>Figures of speech work in <strong>both writing and speech</strong>. Used well, they show command over the language and add to your confidence. The lecturer says they make your language “intelligent”: they add wit, not just humour.</li>\n<li>There is <strong>no complete list</strong> to memorise and <strong>no fixed order</strong> to learn them in. Native speakers pick them up from context. We can do the same by noticing them when we read and listen, and by asking: <em>in what context, and for what purpose, is this used?</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Figure of speech</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Figures of speech are <strong>literary devices</strong> whose function is to present an idea <strong>without directly making a reference to it</strong>.</li>\n<li>They make <strong>comparisons</strong> and draw parallels between otherwise <strong>unrelated</strong> entities or ideas.</li>\n<li>They do <strong>not</strong> provide for <strong>literal</strong> interpretation.</li>\n<li>They are found in both <strong>written and spoken</strong> languages.</li>\n<li>They create a heightened or <strong>rhetorical</strong> effect in speech and writing. (<em>Rhetorical</em> = meant to impress or persuade.)</li>\n<li>They help in using language <strong>creatively</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learning is absorption, not addition</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer says learning a language is not “n + 1”, adding one fact after another. It works by <strong>absorption</strong> and <strong>accommodation</strong>: new things settle into what you already know. So you need not finish grammar before you start on figures of speech (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). In 1.2 he adds that many figures, metaphors especially, are <strong>contextual and culturally grounded</strong>: their meaning depends on the culture that uses them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"figures-of-speech-part-1\">Figures of speech, part 1</h2>\n<p>Lecture 1.1 covers seven figures: simile, metaphor, personification, paradox, oxymoron, antithesis and alliteration.</p>\n<h3 id=\"simile\">Simile</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Simile</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>): “Similes are used in the comparison of two completely unassociated things or ideas. It most commonly uses the words ‘like’ or ‘as’ to draw the comparison.”</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is a simile:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“O my Luve is like a red, red rose” (Robert Burns, <em>A Red, Red Rose</em>; <em>Luve</em> is the poet’s Scots spelling of <em>love</em>). Love is compared with a red rose through <strong>like</strong>.</li>\n<li>“I wandered lonely as a cloud” (William Wordsworth). The poet (<em>I</em>) is compared with a lonely cloud through <strong>as</strong>.</li>\n<li>“He has a simple lifestyle. He is as modest as a hermit.” His modesty is compared with a hermit’s (a hermit lives alone, very simply) through <strong>as … as</strong>. He is not really a hermit. The lecturer adds that it is also an indirect compliment.</li>\n<li>More from the slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>): “A face as dull as lead”, “Cheeks like the blushing cloud”, “Eyes as bright as a blazing star”, “Bold as a hawk”, “As steady as a clock”. Each one links a quality (dull, bright, bold, steady) to an unrelated object with <strong>like</strong> or <strong>as</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: simile</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Simile</strong> sounds like <strong>simil</strong>ar: it says that A is <em>similar to</em> B, and it shows the join with <strong>like</strong> or <strong>as</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Not every “like” makes a simile</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“I like tea” and “as soon as possible” compare nothing, so they are not similes. “Ravi is as tall as his brother” is a plain comparison of two similar things. A simile compares two <strong>unassociated</strong> things, such as a man and a hermit.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"metaphor\">Metaphor</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Metaphor</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>): “A metaphor is a type of comparison in which a word or phrase is used in place of other to suggest a likeness or analogy between them. When we use a word or phrase as a metaphor, it does not carry its literal meaning.” (<em>Analogy</em> = a likeness between two things.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is a metaphor:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“This is the icing on the cake.” It means the better part of something. <em>Icing</em> is the sweet layer on top of a cake, so the phrase stands for an extra good thing on top of something already good. It has nothing to do with a real cake.</li>\n<li>“Silence is golden.” It means the worth of being silent. Silence is called <em>golden</em> (as valuable as gold) without <em>like</em> or <em>as</em>.</li>\n<li>“Life is a roller-coaster ride.” It means alternating highs and lows: good times and bad times. A roller-coaster goes up and down, and so does life.</li>\n<li>“All the world’s a stage, and all the men and women merely players…” (William Shakespeare, <em>As You Like It</em>). The world <strong>is</strong> a stage, and people <strong>are</strong> actors (<em>players</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: metaphor</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A simile <strong>shows</strong> the comparison with <em>like</em> or <em>as</em>; a metaphor <strong>merges</strong> the two things: A <em>is</em> B. The word comes from Greek for “carry across”: a metaphor carries a meaning across from one thing to another.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Simile</th>\n<th>Metaphor</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Says</td>\n<td>A is <strong>like</strong> B</td>\n<td>A <strong>is</strong> B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signal words</td>\n<td><em>like</em>, <em>as … as</em></td>\n<td>none</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is the comparison visible?</td>\n<td>yes, the signal word shows it</td>\n<td>no, you must work it out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lecture example</td>\n<td>“as modest as a hermit”</td>\n<td>“Life is a roller-coaster ride.”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Turn one into the other</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Take away the signal word and a simile becomes a metaphor. “He fought <strong>like</strong> a lion” is a simile; “He <strong>was</strong> a lion in the fight” is a metaphor. (My example.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"personification\">Personification</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Personification</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>): “In personification, a thing or idea is given human attributes and/or emotions. It helps establish a deeper connection with the readers. They make for poetic expressions in speech and writing.” (<em>Attributes</em> = qualities.) The lecturer calls it a special type of metaphor.</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is personification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Books are my favorite companions.” Books are objects, but <em>companions</em> are people who keep you company. The books get a person’s role.</li>\n<li>“The stars winked at us from the distant black sky.” Stars cannot <em>wink</em>; only living beings can.</li>\n<li>A passage from Ernest Hemingway’s <em>The Old Man and the Sea</em>. The old man always thinks of the sea as “her”: a woman who gives or holds back great favours, and who does wild things only “because she could not help them”. “The moon affects her as it does a woman, he thought.” The sea gets a woman’s nature and moods.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: personification</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Find the <strong>person</strong> inside <strong>person</strong>ification: a thing or idea does what only a person does (wink, feel, keep company).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"paradox\">Paradox</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Paradox</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>): “Paradox is a situation or statement that is true but sounds impossible or conflicting. The construction of a paradox consists of two contrasting facts or characteristics. They are used as thought-provoking elements in writing, especially in literature.”</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is a paradox:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Men work together whether they work together or apart.” (Robert Frost, <em>The Tuft of Flowers</em>.) <em>Together</em> and <em>apart</em> contradict each other, yet it is true: people working far apart can still share one purpose.</li>\n<li>“All animals are equal, but some are more equal than others.” (George Orwell, <em>Animal Farm</em>.) Equal things cannot be “more equal”, so it sounds impossible. Its truth: some who preach equality keep extra privileges for themselves.</li>\n<li>“I must be cruel to be kind.” (William Shakespeare, <em>Hamlet</em>.) Cruelty and kindness are opposites, yet a hard act can be done for someone’s good. The lecturer asks you to think about this line several times.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: paradox</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>From Greek <em>para</em> (against) and <em>doxa</em> (opinion): a paradox goes <strong>against</strong> what we expect, yet on second thought it is <strong>true</strong>. Think of it as a puzzle that turns out to be true.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"oxymoron\">Oxymoron</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Oxymoron</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>): “An oxymoron is an expression that contains two words with opposite meanings. They are used to express conflicting opinions or thoughts. An oxymoron combines contradictory words but a paradox combines contradictory ideas.”</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is an oxymoron:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“It was a bittersweet experience.” <em>Bitter</em> and <em>sweet</em> in one word: the experience had both pain and pleasure.</li>\n<li>“That was an awfully good movie.” <em>Awfully</em> (from <em>awful</em>, very bad) sits next to <em>good</em>.</li>\n<li>“Parting with her was a sweet sorrow.” <em>Sweet</em> and <em>sorrow</em> (sadness) are opposites.</li>\n<li>“O brawling love, O loving hate” (William Shakespeare, <em>Romeo and Juliet</em>). <em>Brawling</em> (noisy fighting) goes with <em>love</em>, and <em>loving</em> with <em>hate</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: oxymoron</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The word is its own example: Greek <em>oxys</em> = sharp, <em>moros</em> = dull. “Sharp-dull” is two opposite words side by side.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"antithesis\">Antithesis</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Antithesis</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>): “It involves two components in opposition at the same [time]. The components in opposition follow a parallel structure i.e. they have the same grammatical structure. The parallel structure makes for rhythmic writing and speech.”</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is antithesis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“No pain, no gain.” <em>Pain</em> and <em>gain</em> are opposites, and both halves have the same pattern: <em>no</em> + noun.</li>\n<li>“Man proposes, God disposes.” <em>Man</em> and <em>God</em>, <em>proposes</em> (plans) and <em>disposes</em> (decides what really happens), in the same pattern: subject + verb.</li>\n<li>“To err is human, to forgive divine.” <em>Err</em> (make mistakes) and <em>forgive</em>, <em>human</em> and <em>divine</em> (godlike), in the same pattern: <em>to</em> + verb, then a describing word.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: antithesis</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Greek <em>anti</em> (against) and <em>thesis</em> (statement): two <strong>opposite</strong> statements in <strong>matching</strong> shape, balanced like the two sides of a see-saw.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Figure</th>\n<th>What clashes (quick test)</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Oxymoron</td>\n<td>two opposite <strong>words</strong>, side by side</td>\n<td>“bittersweet”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paradox</td>\n<td>opposite <strong>ideas</strong> in a statement that sounds impossible but is true</td>\n<td>“I must be cruel to be kind.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Antithesis</td>\n<td>two opposite parts in the <strong>same grammar pattern</strong></td>\n<td>“No pain, no gain.”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Oxymoron or paradox?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s one-line test: an oxymoron combines contradictory <strong>words</strong>; a paradox combines contradictory <strong>ideas</strong>. “Sweet sorrow” is two words, so it is an oxymoron. “I must be cruel to be kind” is a whole idea that seems to fight itself but is true, so it is a paradox.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"alliteration\">Alliteration</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Alliteration</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SrYTLUUfrpQ&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>, and again in 1.2 at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>): “Alliteration is a literary device in which the first sound of two adjacent words are repeated. They are more commonly found in poems but, are also found in speech in the form of alliterative phrases. They are also used as company names to create a catchy phrase that is memorable.” (<em>Adjacent</em> = next to each other.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is alliteration:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Betty baked buns besides bacons.” Every word begins with the same <strong>b</strong> sound.</li>\n<li>Brand names: Tiktok, Coca Cola, Paypal, Peppa pig, kitkat. Both parts begin with the same sound (t and t; k and k; p and p; p and p; k and k), which makes the name catchy and easy to remember.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Alliteration as a play of sounds</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In 1.2 the lecturer calls alliteration a <strong>play of sounds</strong>: neighbouring words begin with sounds that are often made at the same place in the mouth and in the same manner (the <em>place</em> and <em>manner of articulation</em> from English 1). He asks you to look at the brand names again with that knowledge, for example whether both <em>p</em> sounds in <em>PayPal</em> are aspirated, that is, said with a puff of air (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: alliteration</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>All</strong> the words <strong>lead</strong> with one sound: “<strong>B</strong>etty <strong>b</strong>aked <strong>b</strong>uns”. It is the <strong>sound</strong> that counts, not the spelling: <em>Coca Cola</em> (c) and <em>kitkat</em> (k) repeat the same k sound.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"figures-of-speech-part-2\">Figures of speech, part 2</h2>\n<p>Lecture 1.2 returns to alliteration, then covers its three forms and nine more figures: irony, pun, juxtaposition, synecdoche, anaphora, metonymy, litotes, hyperbole and euphemism. The lecturer opens with a reminder (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>): figures of speech let us say <strong>more</strong> than we literally say, or <strong>less</strong>. We learn them by noticing how others use them, in published writing and in spontaneous speech.</p>\n<h3 id=\"assonance-consonance-and-sibilance\">Assonance, consonance and sibilance</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>): “Assonance, consonance and sibilance are forms of alliterations. However, unlike an alliteration, the repeating sounds in the words need not be found adjacent to each other or placed in the beginning of the words.”</p>\n<p>These forms are found in poetry and prose. Ad jingles and even news tickers use them as catchphrases, and they give a <strong>lyrical</strong> (song-like) effect to speech and writing. When space or time is short, as in advertising, these sound patterns make a phrase stick (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Assonance</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong>: “In assonance, the same <strong>vowel</strong> sound is repeated in two or more words found close to each other.” (Vowel sounds are the sounds we write with <em>a, e, i, o, u</em>.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>), coloured on the slide:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Jack and Jill went up the hill.” The vowel sound <strong>i</strong> repeats in J<strong>i</strong>ll and h<strong>i</strong>ll.</li>\n<li>“Too cool for school.” The vowel sound <strong>oo</strong> repeats in t<strong>oo</strong>, c<strong>oo</strong>l and sch<strong>oo</strong>l.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Consonance</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong>: “In consonance, the same <strong>consonant</strong> sound is repeated in two or more words.” (Consonant sounds are the other sounds: b, k, l, s and so on.) “When consonance occurs at the end of two or more words, they form a <strong>rhyme</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Jack and Jill went up the hill.” The <strong>l</strong> sound repeats at the end of Ji<strong>ll</strong> and hi<strong>ll</strong>. So this one line shows assonance <strong>and</strong> consonance.</li>\n<li>“Too cool for school.” The <strong>l</strong> sound repeats at the end of coo<strong>l</strong> and schoo<strong>l</strong>.</li>\n<li>Edgar Allan Poe, <em>The Bells</em>: “Hear the loud alarum bells— / Brazen bells! / What tale of terror, now, their turbulency tells!” The ending <em>-ells</em> repeats in <em>bells</em> and <em>tells</em>. That is consonance at the end of words, so the slide labels it <strong>rhyme</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sibilance</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong>: “In sibilance, the sounds /s/ or /sh/ are repeated in two or more words.” (The slashes mean “the sound”, not the letter.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“She sells sea shells on the seashore.” The <strong>s</strong> and <strong>sh</strong> sounds repeat. It is a <strong>tongue twister</strong>, a sentence made hard to say on purpose: “carefully and purposefully made sibilance” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li>“Simple Simon.” The <strong>s</strong> sound repeats.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide also gives “Chips and dips” and “surf and turf” without labels: the lecturer leaves them for you to classify (see Check yourself).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Device</th>\n<th>What repeats, and where</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Alliteration</td>\n<td>the first sound, at the start of words next to each other</td>\n<td>Betty baked buns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assonance</td>\n<td>a vowel sound, anywhere in nearby words</td>\n<td>J<strong>i</strong>ll, h<strong>i</strong>ll</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consonance</td>\n<td>a consonant sound, anywhere (often at the end)</td>\n<td>coo<strong>l</strong>, schoo<strong>l</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sibilance</td>\n<td>/s/ or /sh/, anywhere</td>\n<td>She sells sea shells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rhyme</td>\n<td>consonance at the end of two or more words</td>\n<td>bells, tells</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember the sound family</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Assonance</strong> starts with <strong>a</strong>, like the vowels <strong>a</strong>, e, i, o, u.</li>\n<li><strong>Consonance</strong>: the word <strong>consonan</strong>ce contains <strong>consonan</strong>t.</li>\n<li><strong>Sibilance</strong> <strong>hisses</strong> like a snake: s, sh (Latin <em>sibilare</em> = to hiss).</li>\n<li><strong>Rhyme</strong> is consonance at the <strong>end</strong>: bells, tells.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">One line, two devices</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>One line can show more than one device: “Jack and Jill went up the hill” is assonance (<em>i</em>) <strong>and</strong> consonance (<em>ll</em>). “She sells sea shells” is sibilance, and because neighbouring words also begin with the same sound, it is alliteration too. In this course assonance, consonance and sibilance are all <strong>forms of alliteration</strong>, so naming the exact form is the better answer.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"irony\">Irony</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Irony</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>): “Irony is often situational. It involves a strange, funny, or sad situation where the actual events could turn out to be in exact opposite way than what one expects. Hence, it involves a clash in expected outcome.”</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is ironic:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“My sister gets really scared at the sight of blood. She is a nurse.” A nurse works with wounds and blood every day, so we expect her to be calm.</li>\n<li>“The snowball fight was rescheduled due to heavy snowfall.” A snowball fight needs snow, yet snow stopped it.</li>\n<li>“The proofreader wrote, ‘I’m committed to excellense’.” A proofreader’s job is to catch spelling mistakes, but <em>excellense</em> is misspelt (the right spelling is <em>excellence</em>).</li>\n<li>Spoken examples: a policeman who is scared of a gun; a lawyer who is worried about speaking.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: irony</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Irony = <strong>the opposite of what you expected</strong> happens. Picture the nurse who is scared of blood.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sarcasm is not in this week’s list</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lectures do not define <strong>sarcasm</strong>, but it is easy to confuse with irony. Sarcasm is spoken irony used to <strong>mock</strong> someone: you say the opposite of what you mean, in a mocking tone. The intonation lecture (1.5) names sarcasm as one of the attitudes the voice reveals. The sarcasm column below is my addition.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Irony (this week)</th>\n<th>Sarcasm</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Where it lives</td>\n<td>in a <strong>situation</strong>: events go opposite to what was expected</td>\n<td>in a <strong>speaker’s words</strong>: they say the opposite of what they mean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aim</td>\n<td>none needed; it can be funny or sad</td>\n<td>to mock or hurt someone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Needs a speaker?</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td>yes, usually with a mocking tone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>the nurse scared of blood (lecture)</td>\n<td>“Wow, you’re early!” to a friend who is an hour late (mine)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"pun\">Pun</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>): “It is the use of a word in a way that it has two meanings. The words could be similar in spelling or sounds.”</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is a pun:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Can February March? No, but April May.” <em>March</em> and <em>May</em> are months, and also verbs: <em>march</em> = walk like soldiers, <em>may</em> = be allowed to. Both meanings work at once.</li>\n<li>“So what if I don’t know what apocalypse means? It’s not the end of the world!” <em>Apocalypse</em> means the end of the world, and “it’s not the end of the world” also means “it’s not a big problem”.</li>\n<li>“I always told you, Gwendolen, my name was Ernest, didn’t I? Well, it is Ernest after all. I mean it naturally is Ernest.” (Oscar Wilde, <em>The Importance of Being Earnest</em>.) The name <em>Ernest</em> sounds exactly like <em>earnest</em>, which means sincere and serious.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer adds that a pun lets us say something else by saying something else. When people want to show that a double meaning is on purpose (or is not), they say “pun intended” (or “no pun intended”).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: pun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A pun = one word, <strong>two meanings</strong> at once: “Can February <strong>March</strong>?”</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"juxtaposition\">Juxtaposition</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Juxtaposition</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>): “In juxtaposition, two opposing ideas are placed next to each other in such a way that they highlight the differences or the similarities. It produces a contrasting effect. Both antithesis and oxymoron fall under juxtaposition.”</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is juxtaposition:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“All is fair in war and love.” <em>War</em> and <em>love</em>, two opposite things, are put side by side.</li>\n<li>“It was the best of times, it was the worst of times.” (Charles Dickens, <em>A Tale of Two Cities</em>.) <em>Best</em> and <em>worst</em> sit next to each other.</li>\n<li>“A living dead person.” <em>Living</em> and <em>dead</em> are placed together.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: juxtaposition</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Latin <em>juxta</em> = next to. Juxta-<strong>position</strong> = <strong>positioning</strong> opposite ideas <strong>next to</strong> each other. It is the umbrella: oxymoron (two words) and antithesis (parallel parts) fall under it. The lecturer adds that they are not exactly types of juxtaposition, but similar in nature.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"synecdoche\">Synecdoche</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Synecdoche</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>(Say “si-NEK-duh-kee”.) <strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>): “Synecdoche is the use of a word or phrase in such a way that the part of it can be used to refer to the whole and vice versa.” (<em>Vice versa</em> = the other way round: the whole for a part.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is synecdoche:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“How did the accident happen? He fell fast asleep on the wheels.” The <em>wheels</em> (a part) refer to the vehicle or car (the whole): <strong>part to whole</strong>. Nobody sleeps on the wheels; he fell asleep while driving.</li>\n<li>“How many of you would come for the trekking? Can we have a headcount?” <em>Head</em> (a part) refers to people: <strong>part to whole</strong>.</li>\n<li>“The police were at my uncle’s shop after the shop adjacent to his reported robbery.” <em>The police</em> (the whole department) refers to only a few officers: <strong>whole to part</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: synecdoche</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>S</strong>ynecdoche = a <strong>s</strong>lice for the whole (wheels for a car, heads for people), or the whole for a slice (the police for a few officers).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"anaphora\">Anaphora</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Anaphora</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>(Say “uh-NAF-uh-ruh”.) <strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>): “This figure of speech thrives on the technique of repetition. It is the repetition of a word or sequence of words at the beginning of a clause, a phrase or a related clause. It is seen in campaign slogans and poems.” (A <em>clause</em> is a group of words with its own verb.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is anaphora:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Stay home, Stay safe.” The word <em>Stay</em> begins both phrases.</li>\n<li>T. S. Eliot, <em>The Waste Land</em>: line after line begins with <strong>And</strong>, as in “And fiddled whisper music on those strings / And bats with baby faces in the violet light”.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: anaphora</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Greek <em>ana</em> = again: the same word <strong>again and again at the start</strong>. Alliteration repeats a <strong>sound</strong>; anaphora repeats a whole <strong>word</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Another meaning of “anaphora”</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In grammar, <em>anaphora</em> also names words like <em>himself, herself, themselves</em>, which point back to a noun said earlier. This week it means the literary device.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"metonymy\">Metonymy</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Metonymy</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>(Say “mi-TON-uh-mee”.) <strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>): “Metonymy refers to the use of the name of one thing for another. The name used is closely related to the word or phrase being referred to.” The lecturer compares it with <strong>nicknames</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is metonymy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“The Kiwis played well in today’s match.” <em>Kiwis</em> stands for New Zealand’s cricket team (the kiwi is a bird linked with New Zealand). In the same way, the <em>Kangaroos</em> means the Australian team.</li>\n<li>“The White House wished all Indians on the occasion of Diwali.” <em>The White House</em>, where the President lives and works, refers to the President of the USA.</li>\n<li>“The decision on this scheme has to be finalized by Delhi.” <em>Delhi</em>, where the government sits, refers to the Government of India.</li>\n<li>“In India, we speak many tongues.” <em>Tongues</em> refers to languages (we speak with the tongue).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: metonymy</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Meto-<strong>nym</strong>-y: <em>nym</em> means <strong>name</strong>, as in syn<strong>onym</strong>. Metonymy borrows the <strong>name</strong> of a closely <strong>related</strong> thing: Delhi for the Government.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Figure</th>\n<th>The link (ask yourself)</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Metaphor</td>\n<td><strong>likeness</strong>: is A <em>like</em> B?</td>\n<td>“Silence is golden.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metonymy</td>\n<td><strong>close relation</strong>: is B <em>connected with</em> A, without being similar or a part?</td>\n<td><em>Delhi</em> for the Government of India</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Synecdoche</td>\n<td><strong>part and whole</strong>: is B a <em>piece of</em> A, or A of B?</td>\n<td><em>wheels</em> for the car</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Metonymy or synecdoche?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ask: <strong>is it a piece of the thing?</strong> Wheels are a piece of a car, so <em>wheels</em> for the car is synecdoche. The White House is not a piece of the President; it is a place linked with him, so it is metonymy. A few examples sit near the border. In this course, follow the lecture’s labels: for example, <em>tongues</em> for languages is listed under metonymy.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"litotes\">Litotes</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Litotes</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>(Say “lie-TOH-teez”.) <strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>): “Litotes refer to the use of a negative statement to say something positive. It is a roundabout way of presenting an idea. It is closer to irony in its function.” (<em>Roundabout</em> = indirect.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is litotes:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“She started her business a few years ago. Now I hear that she is filthy rich.” <em>Filthy</em> is a negative word, but with <em>rich</em> it stops being negative: it means very rich.</li>\n<li>“Did you make this dish? Not bad.” <em>Not bad</em> means good.</li>\n<li>“What he said is not without truth.” <em>Not without truth</em> means there is truth in it.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: litotes</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Lit</strong>otes sounds like <strong>lit</strong>tle (Greek <em>litos</em> = plain, small): say a little, often with <em>not</em>, to mean more. “Not bad” = good.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"hyperbole\">Hyperbole</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Hyperbole</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>(Say “hy-PER-buh-lee”.) <strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>): “This figure of speech emphasize[s] a thing by affixing exaggerating elements to it.” (<em>Exaggerating</em> = making something bigger than it really is.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples, and why each is hyperbole:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“It’s raining cats and dogs.” No animals fall; it means very heavy rain.</li>\n<li>“There was pin drop silence in the class.” The room was so quiet that you could hear a pin drop.</li>\n<li>“He is a very funny person. I died laughing at his jokes.” Nobody died; it means I laughed a lot.</li>\n<li>“I have been waiting forever for him to say ‘Yes’.” <em>Forever</em> stands for a very long time.</li>\n<li>“You speak so sweet. I will get a cavity from all the talking.” Sweet words cannot rot teeth; it stresses how sweetly the person talks.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: hyperbole</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Hyper</strong> = over, as in <em>hyperactive</em>: hyperbole goes <strong>over the top</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Hyperbole</th>\n<th>Litotes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Size of the claim</td>\n<td><strong>bigger</strong> than the truth</td>\n<td><strong>smaller</strong> than the truth, often with <em>not</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How</td>\n<td>adds exaggerating elements</td>\n<td>a negative statement for a positive meaning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lecture example</td>\n<td>“I have been waiting forever.”</td>\n<td>“Not bad.” / “not without truth”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effect</td>\n<td>strong emphasis, often humour</td>\n<td>modest and roundabout, close to irony</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"euphemism\">Euphemism</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Euphemism</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>The lecture’s definition</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>): “It is not meant merely for rhetorical effect or embellishments in language. It is a method used to talk about an inconvenient truth or taboo or a possibly offensive matter. Euphemistic construction is used to talk about something unpleasant or embarrassing without directly mentioning the same.” (<em>Embellishment</em> = decoration; <em>taboo</em> = a subject people avoid talking about.)</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture’s examples</strong>: each mild expression replaces a blunt one, so the unpleasant fact is not named directly.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Euphemism</th>\n<th>Instead of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>passed away</td>\n<td>died</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>guest worker</td>\n<td>temporary worker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>downsized (from job)</td>\n<td>fired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>well-fed</td>\n<td>overweight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>outspoken</td>\n<td>bossy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>correctional facility</td>\n<td>jail or prison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rehabilitation centre</td>\n<td>de-addiction units</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bridge courses</td>\n<td>remedial courses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Remember: euphemism</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Greek <em>eu</em> = good (as in <em>euphoria</em>) and <em>pheme</em> = speech: a <strong>good-sounding</strong> word for a bad thing.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Euphemism or litotes?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both are indirect. A <strong>euphemism</strong> swaps an unpleasant or taboo word for a gentler one (<em>passed away</em> for <em>died</em>). <strong>Litotes</strong> uses a negative to say something positive (<em>not bad</em> for <em>good</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Use figures of speech with care</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s last slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zr4aHZ33UHA&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>): “Figures of speech helps in using language creatively. However, one should be sensitive towards the socio-cultural categories to avoid bias in any form.” In other words, think about how a phrase sounds to people of other social or cultural groups.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"which-figure-is-it-a-quick-route\">Which figure is it? A quick route</h2>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Ask these questions in order</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Is something <strong>compared</strong> with an unrelated thing? With <em>like</em> or <em>as</em>: <strong>simile</strong>. Without them (A <em>is</em> B): <strong>metaphor</strong>. A thing given <strong>human</strong> actions or feelings: <strong>personification</strong>.</li>\n<li>Is one <strong>name</strong> used for another? A part for the whole, or the whole for a part: <strong>synecdoche</strong>. The name of a closely related thing: <strong>metonymy</strong>.</li>\n<li>Are <strong>opposites</strong> together? Two opposite words: <strong>oxymoron</strong>. A statement that sounds impossible but is true: <strong>paradox</strong>. Opposites in the same grammar pattern: <strong>antithesis</strong>. Opposite ideas simply placed side by side: <strong>juxtaposition</strong>.</li>\n<li>Is a <strong>sound</strong> repeated? The first sound of neighbouring words: <strong>alliteration</strong>. A vowel sound: <strong>assonance</strong>. A consonant sound: <strong>consonance</strong>. /s/ or /sh/: <strong>sibilance</strong>. The same ending: <strong>rhyme</strong>.</li>\n<li>Is a <strong>word</strong> repeated at the start of clauses or lines? <strong>Anaphora</strong>.</li>\n<li>Does one word carry <strong>two meanings</strong>? <strong>Pun</strong>.</li>\n<li>Does the <strong>outcome</strong> go opposite to what was expected? <strong>Irony</strong>.</li>\n<li>Is the truth <strong>resized</strong>? Made bigger: <strong>hyperbole</strong>. Made smaller with a negative: <strong>litotes</strong>. Made <strong>softer and polite</strong>: <strong>euphemism</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"discourse-markers\">Discourse markers</h2>\n<h3 id=\"what-discourse-is\">What discourse is</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.3 builds up to discourse in four levels (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sounds.</strong> Each language has its own sounds, described by the <strong>place of articulation</strong> (where in the mouth a sound is made) and the <strong>manner of articulation</strong> (how it is made).</li>\n<li><strong>Words.</strong> A small, finite set of sounds combines into a practically <strong>infinite</strong> number of words.</li>\n<li><strong>Sentences.</strong> Words combine into sentences by an underlying “computation”: the rules of grammar.</li>\n<li><strong>Discourse.</strong> Sentences used in a <strong>context</strong>. A discourse needs <strong>at least two sentences</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When we talk, we send meaning through more than sentences: tone, pitch, body language, even the way we sit or stand, and small <strong>markers of discourse</strong>. People who learn a new language (a <strong>target language</strong>) after about 15 years of age pay attention to many things, but not much to these markers.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-discourse-markers-are\">What discourse markers are</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Discourse markers</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discourse markers are essentially <strong>connectors</strong> that join ideas, sentences, or phrases.</li>\n<li>They are common in both speech and writing.</li>\n<li>They are sometimes called <strong>fillers</strong>, but they play a very significant role in the making of a <strong>fluent and coherent</strong> speaker or writer. (<em>Coherent</em> = clear and well connected.)</li>\n<li>In spoken language, they are used to <strong>avoid repetition</strong> of words or phrases, to allow for a <strong>thinking pause</strong>, or merely as <strong>stop gaps</strong> (something that fills a gap).</li>\n<li>In written language, they may show cause and effect, contrast and addition, <strong>chronology</strong> (time order) and sequence, etc.</li>\n<li>In conversation they are <strong>informal</strong> in nature.</li>\n<li>In speech they are mostly found at the <strong>beginning</strong> of the sentence or phrase, as conversation starters. However, they can very well be used to <strong>shift</strong> or <strong>end</strong> a conversation.</li>\n<li>They help speakers express their <strong>mood or attitude</strong>.</li>\n<li>In terms of word class, they could be <strong>conjunctions</strong> (joining words), <strong>adverbials and adverbs</strong>, and <strong>interjections</strong> (short exclamations such as <em>oh!</em> or <em>wow!</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Filler” does not mean useless</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer warns: do not attach insignificance to the word <em>filler</em>. A pause, a repeated word, an <em>um</em> while you reorganise your ideas: these are part of natural, coherent speech, and sometimes a mere pause works as a marker. A little informality is itself a way to make talk impactful. As the lecturer puts it, it takes a lot of effort to look effortless.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-conversation-with-and-without-markers\">A conversation with and without markers</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s scenario is a conversation between two colleagues, Meera and Kshitij (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). The markers are in bold, as on the slide. (ECR = East Coast Road in Chennai; EMI = a monthly loan instalment; 30K = 30,000 rupees; 3BHK = a flat with three bedrooms, a hall and a kitchen.)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Speaker</th>\n<th>Line</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Meera</td>\n<td><strong>So</strong>, I want to tell you something.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kshitij</td>\n<td><strong>Oh!</strong> What is it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meera</td>\n<td><strong>You know,</strong> I bought a villa in ECR last week. <strong>I mean, like,</strong> there is an EMI.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kshitij</td>\n<td><strong>Ah! Great news!</strong> How much do you have to pay?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meera</td>\n<td><strong>Um..</strong> 30K</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kshitij</td>\n<td><strong>Right.</strong> Is that a lot?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meera</td>\n<td><strong>Well!</strong> Honestly, it is.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kshitij</td>\n<td><strong>Anyway,</strong> you have a house of your own now. Congratulations!</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meera</td>\n<td>Thanks! How about you? No plans?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kshitij</td>\n<td><strong>Actually.</strong> I’m seriously thinking about a 3BHK in the SYG Complex.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Meera</td>\n<td>Ah! <strong>Awesome</strong>. <strong>Ok!</strong> I think it is time for the meeting. Tell me more about this when we are done.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kshitij</td>\n<td><strong>Absolutely.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Why the markers matter (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“I want to tell you something” is a correct sentence, but said suddenly it sounds abrupt. <strong>So</strong> opens the talk gently and draws the listener’s attention.</li>\n<li>Plain “What is it?” also sounds abrupt. “<strong>Oh!</strong> What is it?” sounds as if the listener was waiting to hear.</li>\n<li>The lecturer’s advice: read the conversation <strong>with</strong> the markers, then <strong>without</strong> them. Without them it sounds abrupt and artificial: “I want to tell you something.” “What is it?” “I bought a villa in ECR last week. There is an EMI.” “How much do you have to pay?” “30K.” With them, it sounds the way people really talk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture’s analysis of the scenario (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The majority of discourse markers can be used to <strong>begin</strong> a conversation.</li>\n<li>They can be used <strong>on their own</strong>, without a sentence or phrase (<em>Right.</em>, <em>Absolutely.</em>).</li>\n<li><em>You know, I mean, like, erm…, um..</em> can be used to <strong>connect</strong> two segments of the conversation.</li>\n<li><em>Well, OK</em> and <em>anyway</em> can be used to <strong>change the focus</strong> of the conversation, that is, shift the topic to a new one.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Markers come in all shapes: some look like adverbs (<em>actually</em>), some are phrases (<em>on top of that</em>), and some are almost whole sentences (<em>you know</em>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"markers-by-use\">Markers by use</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s lists and examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gEzyD_f706w&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>To express opinions.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Positive</strong>: <em>wow, absolutely, yeah, yep</em>. “Yep. That’s how I like it to be.” “Wow! You look dashing in this tuxedo coat.”</li>\n<li><strong>Negative</strong>: <em>honestly, to be honest</em>. “Honestly, I did not like the movie.” If someone asks how you liked a party and you begin “Do you want my honest opinion?”, they already know you did not like it. Nobody says “Wow, I did not like the movie.”</li>\n<li><strong>Unsure</strong>: <em>I guess so, I suppose so</em>. “Do you think surfing must be banned?” “I guess so. I mean, I need to think about it.” The marker shows that you have no firm opinion yet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The marker tells the listener in advance what kind of sentence is coming, so we do not “leave a lot for imagination”.</p>\n<p><strong>To start a conversation</strong>: <em>well, first of all, to begin with, for starters, at the outset</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“First of all, please have some tea. We can begin the talk thereafter.”</li>\n<li>“To begin with, this property will only be auctioned to the party who is willing to maintain its heritage value.” <em>To begin with</em> also hints that more conditions will follow.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>To add information</strong>: <em>to my knowledge, as far as I know, on top of that, another thing</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“This is used in cleaning dishes. To my knowledge, you can also use it as a floor cleaner.”</li>\n<li>“She is an obedient child. On top of that, she is a very smart girl.” Smartness is added on top of obedience.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Such a marker shows that you are adding to the talk, not interrupting it or rejecting what was said.</p>\n<p><strong>To add emphasis</strong>: <em>actually, and here’s the thing, as a matter of fact, in fact, apparently, I’d go as far as to say, hands down</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“This is hands down the best pizza I have had in my life.”</li>\n<li>“Apparently, he is the one who spilled the beans.” (<em>Spill the beans</em> = tell a secret.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>To give examples</strong>: <em>for instance, for example, you can see this with</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Walnuts are good for the heart health. You can see this with the decrease in LDL it brings about.” (LDL is the “bad” cholesterol.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>To clarify</strong>: <em>you see this?, you understand this?, did you get that?</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“You must multiply the principal amount, the rate of interest, and the time period and divide it by hundred to calculate the simple interest. Did you get that?”</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>To contrast</strong>: <em>that said, at the same time</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“This could be a minor problem. At the same time, we cannot rule out this as a symptom of an underlying disease.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>To conclude</strong>: <em>overall, all in all, in a nutshell, at the end of the day</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Overall, the article tells us that regulation is better than banning anything.”</li>\n<li>“In a nutshell, the taxes may increase at least by five per cent.”</li>\n<li>“All in all, it was a decent seminar.”</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">How to learn them</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Read each example <strong>with</strong> and <strong>without</strong> its marker and hear the difference. Then notice markers when others speak, in videos and in writing (the lecturer points out that he uses many himself). Slowly they enter your own speech and build your confidence.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"rhythm\">Rhythm</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.4 calls rhythm and pitch <strong>prosodic</strong> (or extra-linguistic) features: they belong to the beat and melody of speech, above its single sounds and words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). People also use the two words loosely, as metaphors: “I do not find any rhythm in life.”</p>\n<p>The aim is <strong>communicative confidence</strong>. <em>Communicative competence</em> is knowing the rules and tricks of communication; <em>communicative confidence</em> is being sure that you can use them. That confidence comes from <strong>accuracy</strong>: “we reach fluency through accuracy” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"rhythm-means-flow\">Rhythm means flow</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>In Greek, rhythm means <strong>flow</strong>. In Hindi, it means <strong>taal</strong> (ताल) or <strong>laya</strong> (लय), words familiar from music and dance (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Singers and dancers train hard to master taal and laya. In our own language, rhythm is already built in. We only need to notice how native speakers of English use it to sound emphatic, effective and impressive.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Syllable and stress</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Two terms first, from lecture 1.5 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>syllable</strong> is a unit of a word with a <strong>vowel as its nucleus</strong> (centre). <em>Tell</em> has one syllable; <em>mourn-ful</em> has two; <em>con-duc-tor</em> has three. A word can have one syllable, or 2, 3, 4, 5 and more.</li>\n<li><strong>Stress</strong> is the <strong>prominence</strong> of a syllable: one syllable is said with more force, so it stands out. The lecture calls stressed syllables <strong>prominent</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rhythm</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>All languages have a rhythm. Before we notice their words and sentences, we notice their rhythm. We notice it best when we hear someone speak.</li>\n<li>In music, in songs and dance, we see it best. Each line takes about the same time and contains a nearly equal number of syllables.</li>\n<li>Rhythm in sounds can be compared with the number and patterns of <strong>steps in dance</strong>. Dance arranges steps, music arranges notes, and speech arranges syllables.</li>\n<li>Rhythm is another name for the <strong>repeated patterns of sounds</strong>, an arrangement of syllables, in a unit of speech.</li>\n<li>In a certain <strong>pause-group</strong> (the words between two pauses), we can speak only a certain number of syllables. All of these groups may have one or two more or fewer syllables, but they mostly take <strong>similar lengths of time</strong> to speak.</li>\n<li>They may also have comparable numbers of <strong>prominent</strong> syllables. This arrangement of syllables in a unit of speech is known as rhythm.</li>\n<li>Without this rhythm, the language may sound <strong>monotonous</strong> (on one flat note), like machine-generated speech.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Robots and rhythm</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Films make a robot sound like a robot by taking away the rhythm (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>). The lecturer credits speech technology for what it already does, but says machine speech so far has no natural rhythm. That is one key difference between human (natural) language and machine language.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"english-is-stress-timed\">English is stress-timed</h3>\n<p>David Crystal’s description (<em>How Language Works</em>, 2005), quoted on a slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pitch, loudness and tempo</strong> combine to make up a language’s rhythm.</li>\n<li><strong>English</strong> uses stressed syllables at roughly regular intervals, with unstressed syllables between them: a <strong>stress-timed</strong> rhythm that you can tap out as “tum-te-tum”.</li>\n<li>In <strong>French</strong>, the syllables come in a steady flow, a “machine-gun” effect: a <strong>syllable-timed</strong> rhythm, like “rat-a-tat-a-tat”.</li>\n<li>In <strong>Latin</strong>, rhythm came from the <strong>length</strong> of syllables (long or short). In many <strong>oriental</strong> languages it comes from <strong>pitch height</strong> (high or low).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Language</th>\n<th>Rhythm is based on</th>\n<th>Sounds like</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>English</td>\n<td>stressed syllables at regular intervals (stress-timed)</td>\n<td>tum-te-tum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>French</td>\n<td>every syllable in a steady flow (syllable-timed)</td>\n<td>rat-a-tat-a-tat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latin</td>\n<td>long and short syllables</td>\n<td>not described</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>many oriental languages</td>\n<td>pitch height, high or low</td>\n<td>not described</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tapping out an English line (the line in Crystal’s quote)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide prints the stressed syllables in bold: “The <strong>cur</strong>few <strong>tolls</strong> the <strong>knell</strong> of <strong>part</strong>ing <strong>day</strong>.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Split the line into syllables: the-cur-few-tolls-the-knell-of-part-ing-day.</li>\n<li>Count them: 10 syllables.</li>\n<li>Count the stressed ones: cur, tolls, knell, part, day = 5.</li>\n<li>Read the pattern: weak and strong alternate, te-<strong>TUM</strong> te-<strong>TUM</strong> te-<strong>TUM</strong> te-<strong>TUM</strong> te-<strong>TUM</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 5 beats at roughly regular intervals, with weak syllables between them: a stress-timed rhythm.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"stress-changes-meaning\">Stress changes meaning</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s exercise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>): read the same sentence aloud seven times, stressing a different word each time (in bold). The meanings in the right column are the slide’s.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The sentence</th>\n<th>Implied meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>I</strong> thought your brother was a bus conductor.</td>\n<td>not someone else thought…</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I <strong>thought</strong> your brother was a bus conductor.</td>\n<td>you thought I knew he was a bus conductor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I thought <strong>your</strong> brother was a bus conductor.</td>\n<td>not your friend’s brother</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I thought your <strong>brother</strong> was a bus conductor.</td>\n<td>not your sister</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I thought your brother <strong>was</strong> a bus conductor.</td>\n<td>I didn’t know he still is a bus conductor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I thought your brother was a <strong>bus</strong> conductor.</td>\n<td>before, I thought he was an orchestral conductor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I thought your brother was a bus <strong>conductor</strong>.</td>\n<td>not a bus driver</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Try it aloud</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Same seven words, seven meanings: only the stressed word changes. Try the same with other sentences. Stress, and the rhythm it creates, is part of what a sentence means.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"rhythm-in-poems-and-songs\">Rhythm in poems and songs</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Marking stress in “A Psalm of Life” (the lecture’s activity at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide marks the stressed syllables of Henry Wadsworth Longfellow’s poem <em>A Psalm of Life</em> (bold here) and counts the syllables of the first four lines:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>“<strong>Tell</strong> me <strong>not</strong>, in <strong>mourn</strong>ful <strong>num</strong>bers”: Tell-me-not-in-mourn-ful-num-bers = <strong>8</strong> syllables.</li>\n<li>“<strong>Life</strong> is but an <strong>emp</strong>ty <strong>dream</strong>!”: Life-is-but-an-emp-ty-dream = <strong>7</strong> syllables.</li>\n<li>“For the <strong>soul</strong> is <strong>dead</strong> that <strong>slum</strong>bers”: For-the-soul-is-dead-that-slum-bers = <strong>8</strong> syllables.</li>\n<li>“And <strong>things</strong> are not <strong>what</strong> they <strong>seem</strong>.”: And-things-are-not-what-they-seem = <strong>7</strong> syllables.</li>\n<li>The next lines, with the pauses marked by slant lines: “<strong>Life</strong> is <strong>real</strong>! / <strong>Life</strong> is <strong>ear</strong>nest! // And the <strong>grave</strong> / is <strong>not</strong> its <strong>goal</strong>; //”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the lines go 8, 7, 8, 7 syllables, so each takes about the same time to say. The stressed syllables give the beat, and the slant lines (/ and //) mark the pause groups.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The activity: listen to the poem online (it is freely available), then mark the stressed syllables yourself. The slide marks the rest of the poem in the same way, for example “<strong>Art</strong> is <strong>long</strong>, and <strong>Time</strong> is <strong>fleet</strong>ing”.</p>\n<p>The other activities use two songs (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Doris Day’s song “Que Sera, Sera”, from the 1956 film <em>The Man Who Knew Too Much</em>. Look at the text, then listen to the song a few times, with and without the text, until you have both the words and the rhythm. The answer slide marks the pause groups with / and //.</li>\n<li>A song from <em>My Fair Lady</em>, a musical play later made into a film. Mark its stressed syllables in the same way.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecturer sings a line himself without the intended stress pattern to show the point: if you do not know which syllables carry the stress, the song (or your speech) turns monotonous. The song lyrics are not copied here; find the songs online, as the lecture suggests.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Virginia Woolf on rhythm</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The writer Virginia Woolf, in a letter to Vita Sackville-West (the slide dates it 1928), wrote: “Style is a very simple matter; it is all rhythm. Once you get that, you can’t use the wrong words.” She goes on: a sight or an emotion first creates “this wave in the mind”, long before words come to fit it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>). The lecturer’s point: rhythm goes deeper than words.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"pitch\">Pitch</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pitch</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pitch is an important component of <strong>accentuation, or prominence</strong>, both at the level of individual words and at the level of longer <strong>utterances</strong> (Martha, 1996). (An <em>utterance</em> is a piece of speech: a word, a phrase or a sentence.)</li>\n<li>The pitch of the voice is determined by the <strong>frequency</strong> with which the <strong>vocal cords vibrate</strong>, that is, how many times per second they shake.</li>\n<li>Pitch is a property that allows the ordering of sounds on a frequency-related scale. Pitches are compared as <strong>higher</strong> and <strong>lower</strong>: the voice <strong>rises</strong> to a high pitch and <strong>falls</strong> to a low one.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Pitch carries feeling. Try saying <strong>“thank you”</strong> twice: once flatly, once warmly. The words are the same, but from your pitch the listener can tell whether you mean it or are indifferent (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aJhf0Vp9040&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Low pitch can show</th>\n<th>High pitch can show</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>sadness, shock, dullness, guilt</td>\n<td>excitement, joy, ecstasy, triumph, and even anger</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Using pitch (the slide’s advice)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>well-balanced</strong> pitch results in a clear and effective tone and helps you avoid being <strong>monotonous</strong>.</li>\n<li>Use a <strong>variety</strong> of pitches to hold the listener’s attention.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid raising the pitch at the end of a sentence.</strong> Lecture 1.5 shows why: a rise at the end can turn a statement into a question.</li>\n<li>A pitch that does not fit the context (high where it should be low) sounds odd, let alone impressive. Watch others speak English, and watch yourself speak your own language.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"intonation\">Intonation</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Intonation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Intonation is a feature of <strong>pronunciation</strong>. It shows only when we speak, not in written language (unless a writer marks it specially).</li>\n<li>It refers to <strong>melody</strong>. It gets created by <strong>changing the pitch</strong> of the voice.</li>\n<li>Other such features are <strong>stress, accent and rhythm</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is related to <strong>how</strong> we say something.</li>\n<li>It is responsible for <strong>variation in pitch</strong>.</li>\n<li>Intonations help express <strong>emotions</strong> too.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"five-related-terms\">Five related terms</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s short definitions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>The lecture’s definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stress</td>\n<td>prominence of a syllable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tone</td>\n<td>the manner of sounds; a tone can be <strong>high</strong> or <strong>low</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intonation</td>\n<td>change in pitch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rhythm</td>\n<td>change in stress at intervals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pitch</td>\n<td>relative height of tones as we hear them; it links tones and intonations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>We use these words every day (“I did not like the tone”, “you have an accent”, “it was very rhythmic”) without a clear idea of what they mean (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). They are related, but each does a different job.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rising-falling-and-flat\">Rising, falling and flat</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>There are three types of intonation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>): <strong>rising</strong> (the voice moves up), <strong>falling</strong> (it moves down) and <strong>flat</strong> (level, with no rise or fall).</li>\n<li><strong>Flat</strong> intonation is used mostly for <strong>statements</strong>: declarative sentences, which state something.</li>\n<li><strong>Sentence-final intonation</strong>, the tune at the end, helps decide the <strong>type</strong> of a sentence. A <strong>rising intonation at the end makes a sentence a question</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“John is a doctor”: statement or question? (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Said flat, “John is a doctor.” is a declarative sentence: a statement of fact.</li>\n<li>Now let the voice rise on the last word: “John is a <strong>doctor</strong>?”</li>\n<li>The words and the word order have not changed; only the final intonation has.</li>\n<li>The listener now hears a question: the speaker is questioning the fact that John is a doctor.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> a rise at the end turns the statement into a question.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Did you want to go home?”: one sentence, four meanings (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Said flat, it is still a question: the word order <em>Did you…?</em> already makes it <strong>interrogative</strong> (a question sentence).</li>\n<li>The lecturer then puts the rise on a different word each time: <em>home</em>, then <em>go</em>, then <em>want</em>, then <em>you</em>.</li>\n<li>Each rise puts that word <strong>in focus</strong>, and the meaning changes:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rise on</th>\n<th>What is in focus (my gloss)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Did you want to go <strong>home</strong>?</td>\n<td>home, rather than somewhere else</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did you want to <strong>go</strong> home?</td>\n<td>going, the act itself</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did you <strong>want</strong> to go home?</td>\n<td>the wish: did you really want it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Did <strong>you</strong> want to go home?</td>\n<td>you, rather than someone else</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li>Hearing where the rise is lets the listener <strong>rule out</strong> the other meanings.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> one sentence can carry four meanings, and intonation <strong>resolves the ambiguity</strong> (<em>ambiguity</em> = having more than one possible meaning).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>In the same way, in “Did you know what they said?”, a rise on <em>you</em> stresses the person, a rise on <em>know</em> stresses the act of knowing, and the rise can also spread over the whole clause <em>what they said</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-intonation-does\">What intonation does</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>What intonation does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grammatical</td>\n<td>marks beginning, end, clause, sentence, statement, questions; resolves ambiguity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attitudinal</td>\n<td>helps identify shock, surprise, anger, sarcasm, pleasure, interest, etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discourse</td>\n<td>helps identify the conclusion (we use a different tune at the start and at the end of a talk)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Focus</td>\n<td>focuses parts of the message, as in <em>Did you want to go home?</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychological</td>\n<td>makes things easy to memorise: we remember a sarcastic remark, and the whole event with it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reading between the lines</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Intonation, tone and pitch let us catch what the words alone do not say: the <strong>subtext</strong> (the hidden meaning) and the speaker’s <strong>intentionality</strong> (what they really intend). “We say way more than what sentences can say.” Listen carefully to how people speak (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uE_v3pZFFe0&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"language-use\">Language use</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 1.6 (by Karthika Sathyanathan) looks at language <strong>outside</strong> us: in society, in schools and in India. It gives no grammar rules. Its aim is to clear away myths and stereotypes that stop us from speaking confidently, in any language (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"india-s-language-families\">India’s language families</h3>\n<p>A <strong>language family</strong> is a group of languages that come from a common ancestor. India has five main families (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Examples (lecture)</th>\n<th>Spoken mainly in</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Indo-Aryan</td>\n<td>Hindi, Assamese</td>\n<td>the north</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dravidian</td>\n<td>Tamil, Malayalam, Telugu</td>\n<td>the south</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Austro-Asiatic (Munda)</td>\n<td>Santali, Mundari, Ho, Sora</td>\n<td>in and around Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh and Odisha</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tibeto-Burman</td>\n<td>Tibetic, Tani, Naga</td>\n<td>the Himalayan regions and parts of the Northeast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Andamanese</td>\n<td>Jarawa, Sentinelese, Aka-Kol</td>\n<td>the Andaman and Nicobar Islands</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"languages-borrow-and-mix\">Languages borrow and mix</h3>\n<p>In 2015 the lecturer visited a first-standard classroom in Chennai (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). A six-year-old boy said:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Naa school varumbo chaya kudichu. Two idly kazhichu. Illa. One idly kazhichu, one idly baaki vechu…” (I had tea before coming to the school. I had two idlies… No… I had one idly…)</li>\n<li>“En kitteyum sticker undalo chechi. Kaanano? Ibeda ba.” (I too have stickers, sister. Do you want to see? Come here.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>At first sight these look like Malayalam sentences with a few English words (<em>one, two, sticker</em>). A deeper look shows Malayalam <strong>loan words</strong>: words borrowed from other languages that many Malayalam speakers feel are Malayalam, such as the <strong>Arabic</strong> <em>baaki</em> and the <strong>Hebrew</strong> <em>ba</em>. The slide lists Arabic, Hebrew, Portuguese and English as sources. Languages are open and fluid: they lend and borrow.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reduplication</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Reduplication</strong> is repeating a word to give extra emphasis (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). It is common in Indian languages: Hindi <em>dheere dheere</em> (slowly, slowly), Tamil <em>romba romba mosam</em> and Malayalam <em>valare valare mosam</em> (very, very bad). We carry it into English:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“He is very, very good.” “He is very, very bad.” “He is very, very talkative.” (familiar from parent-teacher meetings)</li>\n<li>“She is walking slowly, slowly.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Native speakers may say “very, very” for emphasis, but they do not reduplicate words like <em>slowly</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>So a rule of one language (Hindi, Tamil, Malayalam) is openly taken into another (English). Languages are <strong>elastic</strong> and <strong>absorptive</strong>: they stretch, and they soak things up. <strong>Multilingualism</strong>, the use of many languages, is the very fabric of our society: we are multilingual, and so are our languages (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"language-in-school-three-myths\">Language in school: three myths</h3>\n<p>The lecture reads two short texts (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>principal</strong> tells the parents of first-graders that some children have not been to pre-primary classes. If a classmate cannot yet write English letters, parents should not separate their child from him, but tell the child to teach the friend: “Let us not follow any kind of discrimination in our school.” Most of us were told to befriend the “studious” children, and students who use good English are still admired. In most private, English-medium and central government schools, regional languages are not allowed among students.</li>\n<li>A <strong>teacher’s</strong> opinion (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>): “This is the era of globalization. English is the dominant language now. The outside world awaiting every kid is fiercely competitive. Without English...you are out of the race. The school should not waste time teaching regional languages that will be of no or little use to the students.” (Many other teachers felt regional languages should be taught; this answer is quoted to show the myths.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The three myths, and what the lecture says about each (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“An early start is the ideal condition.”</strong> Not really. The <strong>quality of the input</strong> (the language the learner receives) matters more. The <strong>Critical Period Hypothesis</strong>, a debated theory, says that children below 14 acquire languages faster than adults. But research shows adults are slower mainly because of <strong>inhibitions</strong>: shame, and fear of making errors. Without them, adults can learn like anybody else. (The slide also names the <strong>Input Hypothesis</strong> here.)</li>\n<li><strong>“The mother tongue stands in the way of learning a foreign language.”</strong> False; the opposite is true. A sound knowledge of the mother tongue <strong>aids</strong> second-language learning.</li>\n<li><strong>Monolingualism: “A child should hear only one language, English.”</strong> False. Multilingualism is the fabric of our society. Our brain is equipped to be multilingual, and multilingualism brings <strong>cognitive benefits</strong> (benefits for thinking).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"english-in-india\">English in India</h3>\n<p>Why did English flourish in India, and not Hindi, Assamese, Sanskrit, Tamil, Marathi, Odia or Malayalam (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>)? English is a global language, linked with success, but the lecture gives more reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>English is nobody’s language.</strong> People see that raising one language to official status gives great benefits to those whose mother tongue it is, and places a <strong>discriminatory burden</strong> on others. So speakers of the non-favoured languages push a foreign language as the <strong>link language</strong>, to neutralise this imbalance.</li>\n<li>A fact to remember: <strong>Hindi is not the national language</strong> of India. Hindi is the <strong>official language</strong>, and English is the <strong>associate official language</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sanskrit, Latin and Greek did not flourish. They were languages of the <strong>elites</strong>: controlled by one class, they became rigid, did not mix, and were not accessible to ordinary people.</li>\n<li>English kept its place through <strong>fluidity, willingness to change and openness</strong>. It travelled across the country and formed variants such as <strong>Hinglish, Manglish and Tanglish</strong>. Mixed with Indian languages, it took a new shape: <strong>Indian English</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"indian-english\">Indian English</h3>\n<p>The features the lecture lists (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Indian English</th>\n<th>The form the lecture gives</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Reduplication</td>\n<td>“She is walking slowly, slowly.”</td>\n<td>no repeated <em>slowly</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retroflex sounds</td>\n<td>the <em>t</em> of <em>table</em> said with the tongue tip curled back (as in Hindi ट)</td>\n<td>the native speaker’s <em>t</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use of articles</td>\n<td>“and one fat man”; “We are going to temple.”</td>\n<td>“and a fat man”; “We are going to a temple” (or “the temple you visited last week”)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tense fronting (slide: “tense (never fronted)”)</td>\n<td>“I know what is your name.”</td>\n<td>“I know what your name is.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preposition stranding</td>\n<td>“To whom did you give it?”</td>\n<td>“Whom did you give it to?”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tag questions</td>\n<td>“It is beautiful, no?” (from Hindi <em>sundar hai na</em>)</td>\n<td>not given</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two cautions</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>In grammar books, both “To whom did you give it?” (formal) and “Who(m) did you give it to?” (everyday) are accepted English. The second one leaves the preposition <em>to</em> at the end, which is what <em>preposition stranding</em> means. The lecture calls the first one wrong; remember its example for this course.</li>\n<li>For tag questions, standard English uses a short tag that matches the verb: “It is beautiful, isn’t it?” (My addition; the lecture does not give the standard form.)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Why do these features appear (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)? Because the <strong>mother tongue</strong> influences any foreign language we speak. The slide sums it up as “learning is unlearning (Multilingualism / First language influence)”. This is natural and normal for every speaker of every language. Do not feel ashamed, and do not fear errors: that only stops you from improving.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Our English is not wrong</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>): “Our English is not wrong. We speak differently. A substantial number of people speak the language in the same way and in the process their foreign language is localized or Indianized. English is thus a part of our linguistic ecology.” (<em>Linguistic ecology</em> = the natural mix of languages around us.) Speak the way that comes to you naturally: “nothing is more graceful” than that.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The one mantra</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The more the input, the better the output” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0XfkFlWWZtk&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>). Expose yourself to English as much as you can: songs, essays, novels, public speeches, news, any piece of writing you come across.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Figures of speech</strong>: literary devices, not meant literally, used for impact.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simile</strong>: compares unrelated things with <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> (“as modest as a hermit”).</li>\n<li><strong>Metaphor</strong>: says A <em>is</em> B, without <em>like</em> or <em>as</em> (“Life is a roller-coaster ride”).</li>\n<li><strong>Personification</strong>: human qualities for a thing or idea (“The stars winked at us”).</li>\n<li><strong>Paradox</strong>: true, but sounds impossible; contradictory <strong>ideas</strong> (“I must be cruel to be kind”).</li>\n<li><strong>Oxymoron</strong>: two opposite <strong>words</strong> together (“bittersweet”).</li>\n<li><strong>Antithesis</strong>: opposites in a parallel structure (“No pain, no gain”).</li>\n<li><strong>Alliteration</strong>: the same first sound in adjacent words (“Betty baked buns”).</li>\n<li><strong>Assonance</strong> (vowel), <strong>consonance</strong> (consonant), <strong>sibilance</strong> (/s/, /sh/): forms of alliteration; the sounds can be anywhere. Consonance at word ends makes a <strong>rhyme</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Irony</strong>: the outcome is the opposite of what was expected (a nurse scared of blood).</li>\n<li><strong>Pun</strong>: one word, two meanings (“Can February March?”).</li>\n<li><strong>Juxtaposition</strong>: opposing ideas side by side (“the best of times… the worst of times”); antithesis and oxymoron fall under it.</li>\n<li><strong>Synecdoche</strong>: part for whole, or whole for part (“wheels”, “headcount”, “the police”).</li>\n<li><strong>Anaphora</strong>: the same word at the start of clauses (“Stay home, stay safe”).</li>\n<li><strong>Metonymy</strong>: the name of a closely related thing (“Delhi”, “the White House”).</li>\n<li><strong>Litotes</strong>: a negative statement for a positive meaning (“Not bad”).</li>\n<li><strong>Hyperbole</strong>: exaggeration (“waiting forever”).</li>\n<li><strong>Euphemism</strong>: mild words for unpleasant or taboo things (“passed away”).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Discourse markers</strong>: connectors, also called fillers, mostly at the start. They begin (<em>so, first of all</em>), connect (<em>you know, I mean, um</em>), shift the topic (<em>well, OK, anyway</em>), show an opinion (<em>wow</em> / <em>honestly</em> / <em>I guess so</em>), add (<em>on top of that</em>), emphasise (<em>hands down</em>), give examples (<em>for instance</em>), clarify (<em>did you get that?</em>), contrast (<em>at the same time</em>) and conclude (<em>in a nutshell</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Rhythm</strong>: the repeated pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in a unit of speech. English is stress-timed (“tum-te-tum”); French is syllable-timed (“rat-a-tat-a-tat”). Stressing a different word changes the meaning.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitch</strong>: higher or lower, set by how fast the vocal cords vibrate. Low pitch can show sadness or guilt; high pitch, excitement or anger. Keep it balanced and varied.</p>\n<p><strong>Intonation</strong>: change in pitch, the melody of speech: rising, falling or flat. Flat for statements; a final rise makes a question. Its functions: grammatical, attitudinal, discourse, focus, psychological.</p>\n<p><strong>Language use</strong>: five language families; languages borrow (loan words) and mix (reduplication); three myths (an early start, the mother tongue as an obstacle, monolingualism); English is the associate official language and a neutral link language; Indian English is a variety, not an error. More input, better output.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<h3 id=\"which-figure-of-speech-is-this\">Which figure of speech is this?</h3>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “The classroom was a zoo after the teacher left.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Metaphor.</strong> The classroom is called a zoo (noisy and wild) without <em>like</em> or <em>as</em>. It is not literal: there were no animals.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “Her smile was as warm as the morning sun.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Simile.</strong> A smile and the sun are unrelated things, and <em>as … as</em> openly shows the comparison.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “The old bus coughed and groaned up the hill.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Personification.</strong> Coughing and groaning are human actions, given here to a machine.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “There was a deafening silence after the result was announced.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Oxymoron.</strong> Two words with opposite meanings sit together: <em>deafening</em> (extremely loud) and <em>silence</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “The more I learn, the more I realise how little I know.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Paradox.</strong> The whole statement seems to contradict itself (learning more, knowing less), yet it is true: learning shows how much is still unknown.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “We came as strangers and left as friends.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Antithesis.</strong> Opposites (<em>came</em> and <em>left</em>, <em>strangers</em> and <em>friends</em>) sit in the same grammar pattern, verb + <em>as</em> + noun, twice.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Alliteration.</strong> Neighbouring words begin with the same <strong>p</strong> sound.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “Go slow over the road.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Assonance.</strong> The same vowel sound (the <em>o</em> of <em>go</em>) repeats in <em>go, slow, over, road</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “He struck a streak of bad luck.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Consonance.</strong> The <strong>k</strong> sound repeats at the end of <em>struck, streak</em> and <em>luck</em>. It is a consonant sound, and it is not at the start of the words.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The slide leaves “Chips and dips” and “surf and turf” unlabelled. Which devices are they?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Assonance and consonance, which together make a rhyme.</strong> <em>Chips, dips</em> repeat the vowel <em>i</em> and the ending <em>ps</em>. <em>Surf, turf</em> repeat the vowel sound <em>ur</em> and the ending <em>f</em>. Consonance at the end of words forms a rhyme. (This answer is mine; the lecture leaves them for you.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “Seven silly swans swam silently seaward.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Sibilance</strong>: the <em>s</em> sound repeats. Because neighbouring words also begin with the same sound, it is alliteration too; in this course, name the exact form.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “The fire station burned down last night.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Irony.</strong> A fire station exists to fight fires; the outcome is the opposite of what we expect.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Irony or sarcasm? “Wow, you’re early!”, said to a friend who arrives an hour late.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Sarcasm</strong>, spoken irony that mocks. The words say the opposite of what is meant, to tease or criticise, and the tone gives it away. Situational irony (this week’s irony) needs no speaker at all.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “Why was the maths book sad? It had too many problems.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Pun.</strong> <em>Problems</em> means maths exercises and also troubles; both meanings work at once.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “All hands on deck!”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Synecdoche, part to whole.</strong> <em>Hands</em> (a part) stand for the sailors (the whole people).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “The pen is mightier than the sword.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Metonymy.</strong> <em>Pen</em> stands for writing and ideas, <em>sword</em> for war and force: closely related things, not parts of them.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “Every day I try. Every day I fail. Every day I learn.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Anaphora.</strong> The words <em>Every day</em> repeat at the start of each sentence: whole words, not just a sound.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “He is no fool.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Litotes.</strong> A negative statement (<em>no fool</em>) says something positive: he is clever.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “I’m so hungry I could eat a horse.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Hyperbole.</strong> A deliberate exaggeration that stresses how hungry you are.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “The company is letting some people go this month.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Euphemism.</strong> <em>Letting go</em> is a mild way to say <em>firing</em>, like the lecture’s <em>downsized</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which figure? “It was the best of times, it was the worst of times.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Juxtaposition</strong>, the lecture’s label: <em>best</em> and <em>worst</em> are placed side by side for contrast. Its parallel structure also makes it antithesis, which falls under juxtaposition.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Metaphor, metonymy or synecdoche? “Silence is golden.” / “Delhi has rejected the plan.” / “He fell asleep on the wheels.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>“Silence is golden”: <strong>metaphor</strong>. Silence is <em>like</em> gold in value (likeness).</li>\n<li>“Delhi has rejected the plan”: <strong>metonymy</strong>. Delhi is a place <em>related</em> to the Government of India.</li>\n<li>“He fell asleep on the wheels”: <strong>synecdoche</strong>. The wheels are a <em>part</em> of the car.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which one is the oxymoron: “an awfully good movie” or “I must be cruel to be kind”?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>“An awfully good movie” is the <strong>oxymoron</strong>: two contradictory <strong>words</strong>. “I must be cruel to be kind” is a <strong>paradox</strong>: a contradictory <strong>idea</strong> that is true.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Hyperbole or litotes? “What he said is not without truth.” / “I have been waiting forever.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>“Not without truth” is <strong>litotes</strong>: it says less than it means (there <em>is</em> truth in it), using a negative. “Waiting forever” is <strong>hyperbole</strong>: it says far more than the truth.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h3 id=\"discourse-markers-rhythm-and-intonation\">Discourse markers, rhythm and intonation</h3>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the use of each marker: in a nutshell, on top of that, at the same time, apparently, for instance, did you get that?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>In a nutshell</em>: concluding.</li>\n<li><em>On top of that</em>: adding information.</li>\n<li><em>At the same time</em>: contrast.</li>\n<li><em>Apparently</em>: adding emphasis.</li>\n<li><em>For instance</em>: giving an example.</li>\n<li><em>Did you get that?</em>: clarifying.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A friend asks how the party was. You begin, “Honestly…”. What has your friend already guessed?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>That a <strong>negative</strong> opinion is coming. <em>Honestly</em> and <em>to be honest</em> introduce negative opinions; positive ones start with <em>wow, absolutely, yeah, yep</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In the Meera–Kshitij conversation, which markers change the topic?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>Well</em>, <em>OK</em> and <em>anyway</em> (the slide’s point iv). Kshitij’s “Anyway, you have a house of your own now” moves from the EMI to congratulations; Meera’s “Ok! I think it is time for the meeting” moves on to the meeting.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">“I thought your brother was a bus conductor.” What does stress on <em>brother</em> imply? And on <em>was</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>Stress on <strong>brother</strong>: not your sister.</li>\n<li>Stress on <strong>was</strong>: I didn’t know he still is a bus conductor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The words are the same; only the stressed word changes the meaning.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">How many syllables does “Life is but an empty dream!” have, and which are stressed on the slide?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Life-is-but-an-emp-ty-dream = <strong>7</strong> syllables. The slide marks <strong>Life</strong>, <strong>emp</strong>(ty) and <strong>dream</strong> as stressed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">How does the rhythm of English differ from that of French?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>English is <strong>stress-timed</strong>: stressed syllables come at roughly regular intervals, with unstressed syllables between them (“tum-te-tum”). French is <strong>syllable-timed</strong>: the syllables come in a steady flow, a “machine-gun” effect (“rat-a-tat-a-tat”). (David Crystal, quoted in 1.4.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Said with a flat tune, is “Did you want to go home?” still a question? What does a rise on <em>you</em> add?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Yes: its word order (<em>Did you…?</em>) already makes it an interrogative sentence. A rise on <strong>you</strong> puts the person in focus (you, not someone else), which rules out the other meanings.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">After you drop a plate, a friend says “Great job!” in a slow, flat voice. Which function of intonation helps you understand the real meaning?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The <strong>attitudinal</strong> function: intonation helps identify attitudes such as shock, surprise, anger, <strong>sarcasm</strong>, pleasure and interest.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h3 id=\"language-use-2\">Language use</h3>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name India’s five language families, with one example each.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Indo-Aryan (Hindi), Dravidian (Tamil), Austro-Asiatic or Munda (Santali), Tibeto-Burman (Naga), Andamanese (Jarawa).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">“She is walking slowly, slowly.” Name the feature and say where it comes from.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Reduplication</strong>: repeating a word for emphasis. It comes from Indian languages (Hindi <em>dheere dheere</em>) into Indian English; native speakers do not reduplicate words like <em>slowly</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">True or false: “A child’s mother tongue gets in the way of learning English.”</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>False.</strong> The lecture says the opposite is true: a sound knowledge of the mother tongue aids second-language learning.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Why did English, and not an Indian language, become India’s link language?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>English is “nobody’s language”: raising one Indian language to official status is seen to favour its speakers and burden others, so a foreign language works as a neutral link. English also stayed fluid and open (Hinglish, Manglish, Tanglish), unlike the elite languages Sanskrit, Latin and Greek.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
